ga7D(){return"Another round"},
ga6o(){return"Draw by insufficient material"},
ga6n(){return"Some positions **cannot be mated** by anyone, however long you play: a lone king, king and knight, king and bishop. The rule ends the game right there instead of making you play on. Remember it before trading everything off while trying to win \u2014 and remember you still have a drawing chance when you are losing."},
ga6m(){return"Take the black rook with your knight. That leaves king and knight, and the game is drawn."},
ga6r(){return"Draw by threefold repetition"},
ga6q(){return"The same position, with the same side to move, appearing **three times** draws the game. In real games this is usually *perpetual check*: the weaker side checks over and over, the stronger side has nowhere else to go, and the game is drawn even though one side is a piece up. It is the lifeline beginners forget they have."},
ga6p(){return"Move the rook back and forth until the position has repeated three times."},
ga6l(){return"The fifty-move rule"},
ga6k(){return"Play **fifty moves each** with no capture and no pawn move and the game is drawn. The idea: if nothing has changed in that long, either the stronger side doesn't know how to win or the position cannot be won. In this lesson the counter only has to reach 12 half-moves \u2014 enough to see how it works."},
ga6j(){return"Shuffle the queen and king around without capturing anything until the counter fills up."},
gE7(){return"Vietnamese\u2013English terms"},
gabu(){return"A quick lookup for reading foreign books and videos"},
